ng all the  points in space at a particular instant  of time we can think of space-time is  kind of a stack of moments in time one  upon another all stacked up into it into  a into a volume it's sort of like taking  the frames of a film and clipping the  frames apart in the film and then piling  them up one frame after the other to  form a solid block that solid block  would kind of contain the film so that  space-time now what is an ordinary  object like a particle look like in  space-time well in each moment the  particle has a location  space there's an event associated with  that particle particle here so the  particle in space-time is all of these  events taken together  I mean Koski says you have what he calls  a world line stretching from the past to  the future that represents a particle  and the motion of the particle is simply  the shape of that world line a particle  at rest it's a straight world line  straight up and down it's parallel to  the time axis at every moment in time  it's the same place of part of the same  location in space a particle in uniform  motion would be a straight world line  but tilted to one side at each moment in  time a slightly different place an  accelerating particle would be a curved  world line a particle moving in a circle  it's world line is a helix a kind of a  spiral reaching from the past to the  future if you had two particles  colliding then there'd be an event of  the collision event where the two world  lines would meet and maybe they'd Bend  and come out in different directions  well suppose instead of just a  point-like particle you had an object  with some size like a baseball or a  planet well then your world line would  have have some thickness what's  sometimes called a world tube so all at  all this is kind of an interesting view  of the world if you will and from the  space time point of view the world does  not exactly happen the past the present  in the future are all combined at once  in space-time all ri